  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R13 supplies the closest precedent: an atomistic, single-crystal study of a closely related FCC CoCrNi alloy varying crystallographic orientation and observing dislocation formation without twinning. R1 establishes MD tensile sweeps over temperature and high strain rate in an HEA, while R6 concerns atomistic stress–strain and CRSS behavior in the very closely related CoCrFeMnNi system. R2 connects strength in FCC Fe–Ni alloys to stacking-fault energetics and dislocation emission. The remaining temperature-dependent HEA studies consistently indicate thermal softening and dislocation-mediated plasticity, and R14 specifically supplies the higher-rate suppression of dislocation annihilation. Thus an expert could reasonably combine orientation-dependent single-crystal MD, temperature/rate variation, and FCC stacking-fault/dislocation analysis for FeNiCrMn. The precise vacancy-cluster nucleation claim and exact Shockley-partial/ISF/ESF sequence are not fully stated in the records, but they are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
